    "evidence_text": "Natural Language Generation for Spoken Dialogue System using RNN Encoder-Decoder Networks\tNatural language generation (NLG) is a critical component in a spoken dialogue system.\n\nThe proposed model was extensively evaluated on four different NLG datasets.\n\nIntroduction\tNatural Language Generation (NLG) plays a critical role in Spoken Dialogue Systems (SDS) with task is to convert a meaning representation produced by the Dialogue Manager into natural language utterances.\n\nproblems.\n\nThe RNN-based models have been applied for NLG as a joint training model  ,  and an end-to-end training model  .\n\nMore recently, Encoder-Decoder networks  ,  , especially the attentional based models  ,  have been explored to solve the NLG tasks.\n\nTo better analyze model\n\nFollowing a detail of proposed model in Section \"Recurrent Neural Language Generator\" , Section \"Experiments\" describes datasets, experimental setups, and evaluation metrics.\n\nRelated Work\tRecently, RNNs-based models have shown promising performance in tackling the NLG problems.\n\nDatasets\tWe assessed the proposed models on four different NLG domains: finding a restaurant, finding a hotel, buying a laptop, and buying a television.\n\nWe assessed the proposed models on four NLG domains and compared to the state-of-the-art generators.",
